 industry\, and contribution to a climate resilient music industry. BEFORE 
 YOU START: you need an approved profile on the Arts Council grants managem
 ent system before you can submit\, so allow time for that. Arts Council re
 serves the right to change the programme\, so check you are on the latest 
 guidance. THIS STRAND: GBP 40\,001 to GBP 100\,000\, with a GBP 2\,000\,00
 0 annual budget for 2026/27 and a 50 percent cash match requirement\, whic
 h is the main thing that will decide whether this strand is realistic for 
 you. You must also upload a completed finance template as well as your mos
 t recent financial statement\; sole traders may submit the finance templat
 e alone. ROUNDS FOR 2026/27 (GBP 1\,000-40\,000 strand): Round 1 opens 6 A
 ugust and closes 3 September 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 2 opens 4 S
 eptember and closes 15 October 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 3 opens 1
 6 October and closes 26 November 2026\, decision 3 February 2027. Round 4 
 opens 27 November 2026 and closes 7 January 2027\, decision 9 March 2027. 
 ROUNDS FOR THE GBP 40\,001-100\,000 strand: Round 1 opens 7 September and 
 closes 1 October 2026\, decision 30 November. Round 2 opens 23 November 20
 26 and closes 15 January 2027\, decision 8 March 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Ar
 ts Council England\nLocation: England. Supports new music by living\, UK-b
 ased artists and music creators.\nAward: GBP 40\,001 to GBP 100\,000\, fro
 m a total annual budget of GBP 2\,000\,000 for this strand in 2026/27. You
  must find at least 50 percent of the total project budget as CASH match f
 unding\, and upload a completed finance template alongside your most recen
 t financial statement (sole traders may provide the finance template only)
 . No application fee.\nDuration: Two rounds across 2026/27\; the next clos
 es 1 October 2026\n\nMore: https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-pac
 kage/music-growth-fund-general-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/music-growth-fund-g
 eneral-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and music
  creators.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:youth-music-trailblazer-fund-round-13-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Youth Music: Trailblazer Fund (Round 13)
DESCRIPTION:Youth Music's Trailblazer Fund gives GBP 2\,000 to GBP 30\,000 
 to organisations in England running projects that let children and young p
 eople aged 25 or under make\, learn and earn in music. A project should do
  at least one of three things: trial work or test a new way of working\, s
 ustain a grassroots programme\, or disrupt the status quo. Doing all three
  is welcome. THEMES: your work must meet one of these: early years\; Disab
 led\, d/Deaf and neurodivergent young people\; the youth justice system\; 
 young people facing barriers\; young adults\; or organisations and the wor
 kforce. WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE\, using Youth Music's own examples:
  a new CIC testing ways to help nurseries embed music-making into the day\
 ; a small grassroots music organisation sustaining a programme that alread
 y works\; a Music Education Hub setting up a Youth Board with real budget 
 and decision-making power over how the hub is run\; or an established yout
 h centre trying a music programme for the first time. ROUND 13: opened 31 
 July 2026\, deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\, notification 11 December 2026
 \, for projects starting between January and March 2027. ROUND 14: opens 2
 3 October 2026\, deadline 20 November 2026 at 5pm\, notification 19 March 
 2027\, for projects starting between April and June 2027. All deadlines ar
 e 5pm. NOTE: this is a grant to ORGANISATIONS\, not to individual musician
 s or composers.\n\nOrganisation: Youth Music\nLocation: England only. Proj
 ects for children and young people aged 25 or under.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to
  GBP 30\,000 for organisations in England. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Round 13 projects start between January and March 2027. Round opened 31 Ju
 ly 2026\; deadline 28 August 2026 at 5pm\; notification 11 December 2026.\
 n\nMore: https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-
 fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.youthmusic.org.uk/funding/i-need-funding/trailblazer-fund?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England only. Projects for children and young people aged 25 or un
 der.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:toronto-arts-council-artists-in-the-library-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:Deadline: Artists in the Library 2027 (Toronto Arts Council)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2014 as a partnership between Toronto Arts Council a
 nd Toronto Public Library\, Artists in the Library gives Toronto artists p
 aid opportunities and space while widening access to the arts in Etobicoke
 \, York\, North York\, East York and Scarborough. It funds professional ar
 tists\, arts organisations and collectives in any artistic discipline to c
 reate and deliver free community-engaged arts programming at a Toronto Pub
 lic Library branch. TAC encourages applications from its equity priority g
 roups and from artists working in their local communities. WHAT RECIPIENTS
  GET OUT OF IT: a chance to explore their practice in a community setting\
 , access to branch space and facilities\, meaningful engagement with the b
 ranch and its community\, close support from the branch team\, experience 
 navigating a multi-stakeholder environment\, and the delivery of a communi
 ty-engaged arts project from start to finish. WHAT COUNTS: community engag
 ement is required but its format can vary\, covering exhibitions\, install
 ations\, performances\, concerts\, screenings\, readings\, filmmaking\, st
 orytelling\, workshops\, artist talks\, participatory programmes\, arts ed
 ucation and mentorship. Permanent exhibitions and installations are not el
 igible. Projects are produced by the recipient\, hosted and supported by t
 he assigned branch\, and must be delivered on site within branch operating
  hours. SCALE: because host slots at TPL branches are limited\, this is no
 t for one-off workshops or small-scale events\; the offering must be subst
 antial with a budget to match. LOCATION: there is no list of eligible bran
 ches for 2026. Applicants indicate a preferred area or neighbourhood outsi
 de the downtown core and TPL staff assign the branch based on suitability 
 and availability\, so a different branch than the one requested may be ass
 igned. For Toronto and East York\, downtown is excluded\, defined as south
  of St Clair\, east of Jane and west of the Don Valley. Only one branch ma
 y be proposed. ELIGIBILITY: individuals must be Canadian citizens\, perman
 ent residents\, have a pending PR application\, or be a protected person\,
  must hold a Social Insurance Number\, and must have lived in the City of 
 Toronto for at least one year before the deadline and live and work there 
 at least eight months a year (no PO boxes). Artists in graduate study may 
 not apply for work related to their programme without a letter from the pr
 ogramme director. Organisations must be incorporated non-profit arts organ
 isations\, and collectives must operate on a non-profit basis\, be located
  in Toronto\, and have both members (for a pair) or a majority of members 
 resident in Toronto\; successful collectives must supply bank account deta
 ils in the collective's name. IMPORTANT: proposals must align with TPL's p
 rogramming policy\, and proposals that TPL staff judge unsuitable for a br
 anch or impossible in its context will not be funded\, so read the 'Workin
 g With Toronto Public Library' section of the guidelines. First-time appli
 cants are encouraged to consult the programme manager and to attend the Zo
 om office hours on eligibility and project fit (17 September) and on budge
 ts (29 September). Contact: Lena Golubtsova\, lena@torontoartscouncil.org.
  Deadline 6 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Toronto Arts Council\, in partn
 ership with Toronto Public Library\nLocation: Toronto\, Canada. Programmin
 g takes place at a Toronto Public Library branch outside the downtown core
 \, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, Toronto and East York\, or Scarborough
 . Applicants must be City of Toronto residents.\nAward: Maximum grant of C
 AD 20\,000\, which may cover up to 100 percent of project costs. Payment o
 f professional artist fees is a requirement of the programme. Recipients a
 lso get access to space and facilities at the assigned library branch for 
 arts programming and presentation\, and support from the branch team. Fund
 ed by the City of Toronto. No application fee is mentioned.\nDuration: Pro
 gramming must take place between September and December 2027\n\nMore: http
 s://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Toronto\, Canada. Programming takes place at a Toronto Public Libr
 ary branch outside the downtown core\, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, To
 ronto and East York\, or Scarborough. Applicants must be City of Toronto r
 esidents.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-in-these-mountains-infrastructure-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: In These Mountains: Infrastructure Grants 2026
DESCRIPTION:Part of South Arts' In These Mountains initiative in Central Ap
 palachia\, these grants fund non-programming operational expenses\, the un
 glamorous infrastructure work that project grants rarely cover. Six organi
 zations receive USD 30\,000 each\, two from each eligible state\, with no 
 matching requirement. WHAT IT FUNDS: staff and volunteer professional deve
 lopment\, emergency readiness planning\, minor equipment purchases or soft
 ware upgrades\, staff travel and registration for conferences or research\
 , marketing\, and strategic planning\, among others. Up to 10 percent of t
 he grant may go to overhead and salaries. ELIGIBILITY: nonprofit organizat
 ions with 501(c)(3) status in good standing located in Appalachian Regiona
 l Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carolina or Tennessee. Colleges 
 and universities are excluded\, as are previous and current In These Mount
 ains Partners from FY19 through FY26. Applications opened 15 July 2026. De
 adline 30 September 2026 at 11:59pm ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocat
 ion: Appalachian Regional Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carolina
  and Tennessee\, USA.\nAward: USD 30\,000 per organization\, with no match
  required. Two organizations from each of the three eligible states are se
 lected\, for six recipients in total. Up to 10 percent may cover overhead 
 and salaries. No application fee.\nDuration: Organizational capacity and i
 nfrastructure support\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunit
 ies/these-mountains-infrastructure-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/these-mountains-infrastr
 ucture-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Appalachian Regional Commission counties in Kentucky\, North Carol
 ina and Tennessee\, USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:south-arts-arts-in-community-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts in Community Grants 2027
DESCRIPTION:South Arts' Arts in Community Grants fund artist fees for commu
 nity engagement projects featuring visiting Southern artists. Each funded 
 project must include both a public presentation and an educational or comm
 unity engagement component\, so this is not a straight presenting grant. U
 p to USD 10\,000 is available per project against a dollar-for-dollar cash
  match\, with a minimum artist fee of USD 4\,000 and a minimum request of 
 USD 2\,000. ELIGIBILITY: tax-exempt 501(c)(3) nonprofit arts organizations
 \, official units of local\, county or state government\, and federally re
 cognised tribal communities with nonprofit status\, located in the nine-st
 ate South Arts region (Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\
 , Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee). Applicants
  must have a Unique Entity Identifier (UEI). Applications opened 15 July 2
 026\; applicants are notified by email by 24 November 2026\, and grants ar
 e subject to funding availability. Projects must take place between 1 Janu
 ary 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline Tuesday 1 September 2026 at 11:59p
 m ET.\n\nOrganisation: South Arts\nLocation: USA\, nine-state South Arts r
 egion: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, 
 North Carolina\, South Carolina and Tennessee.\nAward: Up to USD 10\,000 p
 er project\, covering artist fees. A dollar-for-dollar cash match is requi
 red\, so a USD 10\,000 request needs USD 10\,000 in matching funds. Minimu
 m artist fee is USD 4\,000 and the minimum grant request is USD 2\,000. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Projects must take place between 1 January 20
 27 and 31 December 2027\n\nMore: https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportun
 ities/arts-community-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.southarts.org/grants-opportunities/arts-community-grants?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA\, nine-state South Arts region: Alabama\, Florida\, Georgia\, 
 Kentucky\, Louisiana\, Mississippi\, North Carolina\, South Carolina and T
 ennessee.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:community-engagement-artists-creatives-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Artists and Creatives Grant\, Fall 2026
DESCRIPTION:Community Engagement\, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit based in Orange Co
 unty\, California\, funds independent creatives making or presenting work 
 that relates to or encourages community engagement. Its framing is collabo
 rative placemaking\, projects from communal gardens to public mosaics to i
 ntergenerational art activities that reimagine shared spaces and stimulate
  social engagement\, creative expression and collective empowerment\, with
  a focus on diverse and underserved neighbourhoods. GEOGRAPHY DEPENDS ON T
 HE ROUND: the fall round\, deadline 15 September\, is open only to artists
  living or working in California. The spring round\, deadline 15 March\, i
 s open to artists living or working anywhere in the US. WHAT IT FUNDS: the
  execution of community-engaged projects\, prioritising the creation of ne
 w artworks that present new and imaginative possibilities. The funder says
  it is not likely to fund art classes\, take-home art kits\, or exhibition
 s of studio artwork in preexisting galleries unless the proposal centres a
  clear and purposeful collaboration with a community. WHO CAN APPLY: indiv
 idual artists and artist collectives\, including collaborators. NOT SUPPOR
 TED: arts festivals\, nonprofit and for-profit organizations\, student pro
 jects (undergraduate or graduate)\, residencies\, research symposia\, stud
 io rent\, conferences\, theaters\, and any activity affiliated with religi
 ous organizations. Successful grantees must wait at least two years before
  reapplying. The grant explicitly encourages applications from artists his
 torically underrepresented in the arts\, including Black\, Indigenous\, Pe
 ople of Color\, Queer\, Trans\, Deaf\, Disabled\, Immigrant\, Refugee and 
 Self-Taught artists. HOW TO APPLY: upload four PDFs to a single publicly a
 ccessible online folder (Google Drive\, Dropbox or similar) and submit the
  link via the form\; documents not in PDF format will not be considered. A
 pplications that do not follow the guidelines are not reviewed\, and if yo
 u have not heard back six weeks after the deadline the application was uns
 uccessful. Deadline 15 September 2026 at 11:59pm PST\, with decisions 15 O
 ctober.\n\nOrganisation: Community Engagement\nLocation: California\, USA 
 for the fall round. The spring round (March deadline) is open to artists l
 iving or working anywhere in the US.\nAward: Usually USD 500 to USD 2\,500
 \, and in rare circumstances up to USD 5\,000. The funder suggests most ar
 tists apply for under USD 2\,500 unless advised otherwise. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: Project-based grant\n\nMore: https://www.communityengageme
 nt.org/how-to-apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://www.communityengagement.org/how-to-apply?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:California\, USA for the fall round. The spring round (March deadl
 ine) is open to artists living or working anywhere in the US.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adagp-sgdl-arcane-grant-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arcane Grant 2026 for Artist Books
DESCRIPTION:Artists' books get little support\, financially or in distribut
 ion\, despite their place in art history and in the careers of the people 
 who make them. ADAGP\, the French collecting society for copyright in the 
 visual arts\, and SGDL\, the French society of authors\, created the Arcan
 e grant in response: EUR 9\,000 to one winning project for the conception 
 and production of an artist's book made collaboratively by a visual artist
  and a writer. WHO CAN APPLY: a pair consisting of a professional visual a
 rtist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at least fi
 ve years\, or a French national living abroad) and a French-language autho
 r working in fiction\, non-fiction\, poetry\, children's literature or the
 atre who has already been published by a publisher. A trio is also possibl
 e where a publishing structure joins the project. WHAT COUNTS AS AN ARTIST
 'S BOOK: a creation\, a work of art in book form\, in a limited print run 
 of between 1 and 500 copies\, which may be signed or numbered. Explicitly 
 not eligible: magazines\, fanzines\, limited editions of an ordinary book\
 , and any publication that simply presents an artist's work\, such as mono
 graphs or illustrated books. APPLICATION: the file must be in French and i
 ncludes a jointly written project presentation of up to three pages coveri
 ng the artistic and editorial vision\, constituent elements\, methods of r
 ealisation and desired final form\; a one-page technical sheet giving titl
 e\, print run\, format\, page count\, paper quality\, printing techniques 
 and materials\; a provisional timetable\; and a detailed balanced provisio
 nal budget of expenses and financing. Artistic elements are a CV of exhibi
 tions and publications (2 pages) and a portfolio with website link (10 pag
 es) for the artist\, a CV and website link for the author (2 pages)\, and 
 a one-page editorial line for the publishing structure if there is one\; c
 ontact details must be omitted from these. Administrative elements are swo
 rn declarations that the project is a new creation with copyright held sol
 ely by the pair\, that the artist is from the French scene\, and\, if appl
 icable\, that the publishing structure undertakes to publish the work. Tem
 plates are downloadable from the Required Documents page. TIMELINE: call l
 aunched 7 July 2026\, applications close 7 September 2026 at 2pm\, jury me
 ets November 2026\, winner announced end of November 2026\, and the work m
 ust be published within two years of that announcement. The regulations mu
 st be read before applying\, and submitting an application constitutes acc
 eptance of them. Contact: arcane@adagp.fr.\n\nOrganisation: ADAGP and SGDL
  (Societe des Gens de Lettres)\nLocation: France. Open to a professional v
 isual artist from the French scene (working or residing in France for at l
 east five years\, or of French nationality living abroad) paired with a Fr
 ench-language author.\nAward: EUR 9\,000 to the single winning project\, s
 upporting the conception and production of an artist's book. NOTE ON TAX: 
 ADAGP grants count as primary artistic income under the French Intellectua
 l Property Code (art. L112-2)\, so the sum falls under the artist-author s
 ocial security and tax regime\, subject to URSSAF contributions and income
  tax declared as non-commercial profits (micro-BNC or controlled declarati
 on). VAT is not due. No application fee.\nDuration: The winning duo or tri
 o commits to publishing the artist's book within two years of the announce
 ment\n\nMore: https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=pr
 ogramme&cid=187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://candidatures.adagp.fr/en/?option=com_emundus&view=programme&cid
 =187&Itemid=1531&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:France. Open to a professional visual artist from the French scene
  (working or residing in France for at least five years\, or of French nat
 ionality living abroad) paired with a French-language author.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:linz-kultur-arbeitsstipendien-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Linz Kultur Working Grants 2026 (Arbeitsstipendien)
DESCRIPTION:The city of Linz awards project-related working grants to susta
 in and develop its art scene\, in line with the Linz cultural development 
 plan and the city's status as a UNESCO City of Media Arts. The stated aims
  are enabling the further development of artistic practice\, supporting ar
 tistic careers\, and securing a professional artistic existence and innova
 tive production in Linz. Each scholarship is worth EUR 5\,000 over five mo
 nths\, with EUR 30\,000 available in total this year. DISCIPLINES: fine ar
 ts and photography\, literature\, music\, performing arts\, interdisciplin
 ary art forms\, media art and film. WHAT TO SUBMIT: a concrete artistic pr
 oject\, which may be an independent work\, a series of works\, or the cont
 inuation of an earlier series or project. Preparatory work and concept pha
 ses are also eligible. Projects already funded by the City of Linz cannot 
 be submitted\, and only one submission per person per year is permitted. E
 LIGIBILITY: artists of any citizenship who can demonstrate that their cent
 re of work is in Linz\, in the form of a permanent site for their artistic
  work\, and who have a sufficient connection to the city. SELECTION CRITER
 IA: the expert jury assesses sufficient Linz connection\, innovative artis
 tic potential\, independent artistic expression\, a clearly recognisable c
 onception of the artistic intention in the proposal\, and the quality and 
 continuity of the artistic work. REPORTING OBLIGATION: within three months
  of the scholarship ending\, a written progress report of 1\,800 to 5\,400
  characters including spaces must be submitted with supporting documentati
 on\, and after review it is published on the City of Linz website. The pro
 ject need not be finished\, but significant progress must be evident. If n
 o report is submitted\, or the report shows insufficient progress\, the sc
 holarship must be repaid under the City of Linz General Funding Guidelines
 . REQUIRED DOCUMENTS: the completed online application form\, a project de
 scription of up to 10 pages including the innovative approach and planned 
 schedule\, a biography with catalogue of works and any exhibition or prese
 ntation list\, an artistic portfolio\, and references and press reviews. N
 OTE: the online form and the call documents are in German. Contact: lkf.kb
 @mag.linz.at. Deadline 7 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: City of Linz (Li
 nz Kultur\, Arts and Cultural Funding Department)\nLocation: Linz\, Austri
 a. Open to artists of any citizenship whose centre of work is in Linz.\nAw
 ard: EUR 5\,000 per scholarship over a five-month period\, from a total po
 t of EUR 30\,000 this year\, so approximately six grants. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Five months\n\nMore: https://www.linz.at/serviceguide/viewc
 hapter.php?chapter_id=123406&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
URL:https://www.linz.at/serviceguide/viewchapter.php?chapter_id=123406&utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Linz\, Austria. Open to artists of any citizenship whose centre of
  work is in Linz.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultural-horizons-residencies-subgrants-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260829T072942Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultural Horizons Residencies: Subgrants for EU-Ukrainian
  Residency Projects 2027
DESCRIPTION:Cultural Horizons strengthens the cultural sector in Ukraine by
  promoting joint EU-Ukrainian cultural initiatives and expanding access fo
 r Ukrainians affected by the war to cultural and artistic networks in Euro
 pe. Running 2026 to 2027\, it provides subgrants for projects developed th
 rough EU-Ukrainian partnerships and builds a Europe-wide network of cultur
 al expert mentors. THIS CALL\, the Residencies strand\, gives subgrants to
  EU-based institutions to run (a) residency placements and/or (b) educatio
 n projects organised on the basis of residencies\, for and with Ukrainian 
 artistic and cultural professionals during 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artistic a
 nd cultural organisations and initiatives registered within the EU. Consor
 tium applications are allowed but must designate one lead organisation to 
 submit and manage the funding. This is an organisational call\, so individ
 ual Ukrainian artists cannot apply directly\; they participate as the host
 ed residents. Applicants must show they can host Ukrainian professionals i
 n 2027 and complete the project in the stated window. NGOs and public sect
 or institutions working with displaced and vulnerable groups are prioritis
 ed. CONDITIONS: applicants must confirm the absence of ties with the Russi
 an Federation\, meaning no working partnerships with Russia or Russian org
 anisations and no visits to Russian-occupied territories after 2014\, comm
 it to refrain from the glorification of Russian imperialism\, and comply w
 ith the EU sanctions regime. They must also demonstrate a commitment to di
 versity\, equity and inclusion and provide a working environment based on 
 mutual trust\, awareness and intercultural sensitivity. WHO THE MONEY IS M
 EANT TO REACH: Ukrainian artists and cultural practitioners\, whether inde
 pendent or employed\, including early-career specialists\, debut artists\,
  cultural managers\, researchers\, analysts and conservators\; vulnerable 
 groups from Ukraine including internally displaced persons\, young artists
 \, and communities in frontline and conflict-affected areas\; and cultural
  organisations in Ukraine\, with particular focus on small communities\, l
 ocal initiatives\, independent cultural spaces and NGOs. APPLYING: submiss
 ion is through a JotForm hosted by Cultural Vistas and must be complete\, 
 including uploads\, or it will not be processed. You should receive an ema
 il confirmation\; if it does not arrive\, the submission was not received.
  Enquiries to culturalhorizons@culturalvistas.eu. NOTE ON DATES: the eligi
 bility section says projects must be completed within January to August 20
 27 while the application instructions give the implementation phase as 1 J
 anuary to 30 July 2027\, so plan to the earlier end date. Programme backgr
 ound is at culturalvistas.eu under Our Programs\, Professional Study Tours
 \, Cultural Horizons.\n\nOrganisation: Ukrainian Cultural Foundation and C
 ultural Vistas\, funded by the Creative Europe programme of the European U
 nion\nLocation: Applicant organisations must be registered in the EU. Resi
 dencies are hosted in the EU for Ukrainian artistic and cultural professio
 nals.\nAward: EUR 5\,000 to 20\,000 per subgrant\, with a minimum of 20 su
 bgrants to be awarded. No application fee.\nDuration: Projects run between
  1 January and 30 July 2027\, minimum 3 months and maximum 8 months. Appli
 cations 29 June to 30 September 2026\; evaluation October to November 2026
 . Final reports are due within one month of project completion and no late
 r than 15 August 2027.\n\nMore: https://culturalvistas.jotform.com/2617534
 20662859?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culturalvistas.jotform.com/261753420662859?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant organisations must be registered in the EU. Residencies 
 are hosted in the EU for Ukrainian artistic and cultural professionals.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
